WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.300
TCP gives the application a byte stream.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.960
It does not preserve application-level messages, records, commands, or objects.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.780
A sender may write one logical message and the receiver may read it in pieces.

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.400
A receiver may also read bytes from multiple logical messages in one operation.

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.860
The service must define its own framing before it can parse safely.

6
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:31.260
Framing can use delimiters, fixed headers, length prefixes, structured records, or another documented contract.

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:38.640
The important requirement is that the parser knows where one message ends before it treats the content as complete.

8
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:45.700
A parser that assumes one read equals one message is relying on behavior TCP does not provide.

9
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.180
Partial reads and partial writes should be ordinary cases in the design.

10
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.560
Read logic needs to accumulate enough bytes for the next frame without exceeding buffer limits.

11
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.340
Write logic needs to handle the possibility that only part of a response was accepted by the network stack.

12
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.480
Both directions need state that can resume safely.

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.080
Maximum sizes are part of the protocol.

14
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:14.980
A length prefix without a maximum can turn a simple parser into an unbounded allocation request.

15
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.160
A delimiter without a maximum can leave the service buffering indefinitely.

16
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:30.220
The service should define limits for line length, frame length, field length, header size, body size, queued output, and total per-connection memory where applicable.

17
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.340
Boundary validation should happen before deeper processing.

18
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.800
Delimiters should be accepted only in the intended context.

19
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:45.020
Length prefixes should be converted carefully, checked against minimum and maximum sizes, and validated against the available buffer.

20
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.960
A frame that violates the contract should be rejected cleanly without carrying partial assumptions forward.

21
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.960
The protocol contract should be written well enough that implementation and tests can follow it.

22
00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:08.540
Reviewers should be able to compare parser assumptions to the contract: how messages are framed, what sizes are allowed, what happens when a frame is incomplete, and how malformed input is rejected.
